course: dictionary
lesson: 10312
title: Words for groups of people
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Townspeople means the people who live in a town, thought of all together as one group. The -s on people and the word all together tell you it is a group, not just one person. «Many towns in Ukrainian lands, such as Lviv and later Kyiv, received Magdeburg rights, which let townspeople run their own courts and councils.» Here the townspeople are the ordinary people of those towns.
explain: Look at the words around the word for clues. «The townspeople gathered in the square.» You know they came together in one place, and the people who live in the town are the ones who gather there. Check by swapping in your meaning: "the people who live in a town gathered in the square" still makes sense, so you are on the right track.
explain: Townspeople are a different group from farmers. «Most Ukrainians descend from peasants and townspeople» shows that peasants and townspeople are two separate groups. Words like and, or, though and but often show that two things are being compared or set apart.
fact: Townspeople means the people who live in a town, especially when they are thought of all together.
fact: Townspeople is a plural word: one person is a townsperson, and many people are townspeople.
fact: In stories, the townspeople may cheer, panic or refuse to pay, as in «The Pied Piper of Hamelin».
fact: In history, townspeople were often merchants and craftspeople, different from farmers or lords.
fact: «Many towns received Magdeburg rights, which let townspeople run their own courts and councils.»
exercise: In the story, «the townspeople gathered in the square.» Who gathered? | *The people who live in the town | Farmers from the fields | Wild animals from the forest | The lords who live in the castle
exercise: What does townspeople mean? | Animals who live in towns | Farmers who work out on the land | Soldiers who guard the castle gates | *People who live in a town, thought of all together
exercise: In history, townspeople were often merchants and craftspeople. How were they different from farmers or lords? | They were the same as farmers in every way | *They were a different group, living and working in towns | They lived only in the countryside | They were always kings and queens
test: «Many towns received Magdeburg rights, which let townspeople run their own courts and councils.» What did the rights let townspeople do? | *Run their own courts and councils | Live in castles on the hill | Pay no taxes ever | Herd sheep on the hillside
test: Which word is the plural of townsperson? | Townsperson's | Towners | Townperson | *Townspeople
exam: In «most Ukrainians descend from peasants and townspeople», why are peasants and townspeople named as two different groups? | Because peasants are kings and queens | Because townspeople are animals | *Because they are two different groups of people | Because they mean exactly the same thing
